# vtium's configuration, installed as /etc/kmscon/kmscon.conf.example.
# Generated from crates/vtium/src/config/keys.rs; docs/config.md describes each key.
#
# One key a line: key=value, or a switch's key to turn it on and no-key to
# turn it off. Each key is shown at its default, commented out. The command
# line wins over this file.

## The VT to run on: a number N for `/dev/ttyN`, a name under `/dev`, or a path. Unset, the VT vtium was started on, or the next free one.
#vt=

## Switch to vtium's VT at start; off, wait until something else switches to it.
#switchvt

## The session's command. Unset, `/bin/login -p` with `reset-env` on and `/bin/login` with it off. A word that is `$NAME` becomes that variable of the session's environment, and `$$` starts a word with `$`.
#login=

## Exit when the session's command exits, in place of starting it again.
#no-oneshot

## `TERM` in the session's environment.
#term=kmscon

## Start the session with an empty environment and the variables vtium sets; off, with vtium's own environment as well.
#reset-env

## The pty's erase character is DEL (0x7f); off, BS (0x08).
#backspace-delete

## Lines of scrollback, at most `sb-size-cap`.
#sb-size=1000

## The most lines `sb-size` may ask for. Unset, it follows the machine's memory: 10,000 lines below 2 GiB, 30,000 from 2 GiB and 100,000 from 4 GiB.
#sb-size-cap=

## The most bytes the scrollback holds, or KiB, MiB or GiB with `K`, `M` or `G` (`6M`), at most a quarter of the machine's memory, or the default when `/proc/meminfo` cannot be read; its oldest lines go past it, as past `sb-size`. The default keeps the worst scrollback a program can write inside the 16 MiB minspec peak.
#sb-memory=5963776

## Blink text with SGR 5 and a blinking cursor, every 500 ms; off, neither blinks.
#blink

## How text with SGR 5 or 6 draws in the blink's off phase: `full` hides it, `partial` draws it halfway to its background as SGR 2 does, and `off` draws it steady. The cursor blinks as `blink` says either way.
#blink-text=full

## The underline and strike of blinking text blink with it: hidden in the off phase with `blink-text=full`, dimmed with `partial`. Off, they stay while the text blinks.
#no-blink-decorations

## The longest a synchronized frame (mode 2026) is held before it is shown as drawn; 0 offers no mode 2026.
#sync-timeout=200

## Turn the displays off after this long with no key press or pointer action (a move, a button or the wheel); 0 never does.
#dpms-timeout=600

## Let a program set the blank's timeout with the Linux console's `CSI 9 ; n ]` (n minutes, at most 60; 0 never blanks) and wake the displays with `CSI 13 ]`, as `setterm --blank` does under `TERM=linux`; RIS returns the timeout to `dpms-timeout`. Off, the sequences are ignored. A key press or pointer action wakes the displays either way.
#program-blank

## At start, how long to wait for a display, counted from when the VT is first shown; with none by then vtium exits with status 1, so the unit's `OnFailure=` can start a getty. 0 waits for no display to come: a display set up when the VT is first shown counts once its first frame is on screen. `forever` waits for the first display however long it takes, for a machine whose only display is a USB adapter (DisplayLink, `udl`) plugged in at any time. Once a display has come, vtium never exits for want of one.
#display-wait=10

## Draw through the GPU, in a build that has it.
#no-hwaccel

## Keep the mode each display is in; off, its preferred mode.
#no-use-original-mode

## A display's mode, `WxH[@RATE]`, for every display, `[CONNECTOR]WxH` for one connector, or `GPU[CONNECTOR]WxH` for one on one card, its udev `ID_PATH`. A key per line; the most specific match wins. Unset, the panel's preferred mode. It turns `use-original-mode` off.
#mode=

## The sixteen colors and the default foreground and background: a named palette, or `custom`, which takes the `palette-*` keys. Colors 16 to 255 are xterm's in every palette.
#palette=legacy

## Color 0 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-black=0,0,0

## Color 1 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-red=205,0,0

## Color 2 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-green=0,205,0

## Color 3 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-yellow=205,205,0

## Color 4 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-blue=0,0,238

## Color 5 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-magenta=205,0,205

## Color 6 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-cyan=0,205,205

## Color 7 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-grey=229,229,229

## Color 8 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-dark-grey=127,127,127

## Color 9 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-red=255,0,0

## Color 10 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-green=0,255,0

## Color 11 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-yellow=255,255,0

## Color 12 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-blue=92,92,255

## Color 13 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-magenta=255,0,255

## Color 14 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-cyan=0,255,255

## Color 15 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-white=255,255,255

## The default foreground with `palette=custom`.
#palette-foreground=229,229,229

## The default background with `palette=custom`.
#palette-background=0,0,0

## The color every underline draws in, SGR 4 in each of its styles and SGR 21: `fg` draws it in the text's color, a palette index in that palette color, which follows a program's OSC 4, and `#rrggbb` in that color. A color a program sets with SGR 58 wins unless `force-decoration-colors` is on.
#underline-color=fg

## The color the strike, SGR 9, draws in, as `underline-color` takes it.
#strike-color=fg

## `underline-color` wins over the underline color a program sets with SGR 58.
#no-force-decoration-colors

## Take the selection a program sets with OSC 52; off, it is dropped. The console has no system clipboard: vtium holds the last selection taken, as it holds one the user copies, and the middle button and `grab-paste` paste it.
#clipboard-write

## The keyboard model, as xkeyboard-config names it.
#xkb-model=

## The keyboard layout, such as `us` or `de`.
#xkb-layout=

## The layout's variant.
#xkb-variant=

## xkb options, comma-separated.
#xkb-options=

## A keymap file, taken before the names above.
#xkb-keymap=

## A compose table, taken before the locale's.
#xkb-compose-file=

## How long a held key waits before it repeats; 0 takes 250.
#xkb-repeat-delay=250

## The time between a held key's repeats; 0 takes 50.
#xkb-repeat-rate=50

## Take pointers: mice, touchpads and touchscreens.
#mouse

## Draw the pointer in software on every display, never on a display's cursor plane.
#no-soft-cursor

## Draw the pointer as this character's glyph from the font, one cell wide, on the cell under the pointer, white with a dark outline as the arrow is. Unset, or a character the font lacks or whose glyph is blank, the pointer is the arrow.
#pointer-char=

## Hide the pointer after this long with no pointer event, until it next moves; 0 never hides it.
#pointer-hide-timeout=20

## Hide the pointer at each key sent to the program, until it next moves. A key vtium takes, such as a grab, leaves it.
#no-pointer-hide-while-typing

## Paste the selection buffer to the program, once a press, as the middle button does. A chord with Shift names the keysym Shift makes: `<Ctrl><Shift>V`, not `<Ctrl><Shift>v`. Empty, no key pastes.
#grab-paste=<Shift>Insert

## Reboot the machine. With `/proc/sys/kernel/ctrl-alt-del` at 0, init is asked, as for Ctrl+Alt+Del, so the services stop first; otherwise the kernel restarts at once. Unset, no key reboots.
#grab-reboot=

## Power the machine off: init is asked with `SIGRTMIN+4`, which systemd takes as `poweroff.target`, so the services stop and the filesystems are unmounted first. Unset, no key powers off.
#grab-power-off=

## Suspend the machine to RAM: logind is asked with its `Suspend` method, so its inhibitors hold the sleep; where logind is not running, or vtium is built without `logind`, `mem` is written to `/sys/power/state`. Unset, no key suspends.
#grab-suspend=

## The font source to use. Unset, each the build has, in this order.
#font-engine=

## The font: a set in the store, by its name or by its first source, so a kbd font's name, or with `font-engine=psf` a PSF font's path, finds the set `vtium-vitrify create --kbd` or `--file` built from it. Unset, vconsole's font; with none, or a name with no set built, vtium draws its built-in sets, and logs the `create` command that builds a named one.
#font-name=monospace

## The font's height; 0 picks one from the display's size.
#font-size=0

## Let a program switch the font among the built sets with xterm's OSC 50 (`OSC 50 ; NAME ST`, as `vtium-vitrify set NAME` sends it) and ask which set is in use (`OSC 50 ; ? ST`). On by default, as xterm's `allowFontOps` is, since OSC 50 reaches only the sets root built into the store and the built-in ones, never a file. A switch changes the live console only; `font-name` decides the font at the next start. Off, vtium ignores OSC 50, the query included.
#allow-font-ops

## Where the six sprite ranges draw from: `font`, the font's glyphs; `sprites`, vtium's; `auto`, the font's where it has one. Unset, a named font's glyphs for each range it covers whole and the sprites for the rest; the built-in unifont draws the sprites.
#glyph-source=

## `glyph-source` for box drawing, U+2500-U+257F, over it.
#glyph-source-box=

## `glyph-source` for block elements, U+2580-U+259F, over it.
#glyph-source-block=

## `glyph-source` for Braille, U+2800-U+28FF, over it.
#glyph-source-braille=

## `glyph-source` for sextants, U+1FB00-U+1FB3B, over it.
#glyph-source-sextants=

## `glyph-source` for octants, U+1CD00-U+1CDE5, over it.
#glyph-source-octants=

## `glyph-source` for the rest of Symbols for Legacy Computing's block shapes, over it.
#glyph-source-legacy=

## What a character the font lacks draws as: `box`, U+25A1; `hex`, its codepoint in hex digits.
#missing=box

## The glyph cache's budgets. Unset, `minspec`, or `performance` in a build with vtium-render's `performance-cache` feature.
#glyph-cache=

## Each display's depth. `1` draws it in black and white, each cell lit or unlit by its colors, never by their brightness; `gray4` and `gray16` record a gray panel of 4 or 16 levels, drawn as color, which the panel converts; `color` draws its colors; `auto` takes the levels the display says it shows: an fbdev device at 1 bit, or a DRM panel whose driver vtium-platform lists.
#display-depth=auto

## Measure each display's frames once a second, and write the figures to the log, draw them on screen, or both.
#render-timer=none
